# The zero pipeline's declared delta: what each phase changes, in advance.
#
# Read by tools/zerodelta.sh --phase N, which takes every line up to phase N and
# checks the binary moved in exactly that way -- and nothing else (ZERO-GOAL.md,
# rule 2).  The grammar is pipes/whim.delta's, one line per phase that changes the
# list, continuation lines indented:
#
#   word             an Ex command name: its block in ref-excmds.txt now differs
#   case:NAME        a screen case (tools/zcases.py) whose record now differs
#   argv:NAME        an invocation (tools/zargv.py); spaces are written as `_`
#   term-moved       the terminal table (tools/ztermcheck.py) now differs
#   pty-moved        the pty scenarios (tools/zpty.py) now differ
#   screen-moved     what the editor DRAWS differs everywhere -- the snapshots and
#                    the stream digest of every case, the text and message lines of
#                    every command row.  Exit status, bells and stderr must still
#                    match, case by case
#   stderr-moved     what the editor writes to STDERR differs everywhere, and
#                    nothing else does
#   drop:X           X was declared earlier and no longer differs
#
# The two `-moved` dimensions are not a way of saying "some things changed".  Each
# excludes ONE dimension from every comparison and must itself have moved
# somewhere: a phase that declares `screen-moved` and draws the same screens fails
# (proven, by declaring it here and watching tools/zcompare.py refuse).  Everything
# outside the declared dimension is still checked record by record.
#
# THE DIFFERENCE IS FROM WHIM-VIM, not from slim-vim.  The baselines it is measured
# against are .reference/zero-baselines, which zero phase 0 records from the
# committed whim-vim.c built with whim's own compile line.  So everything whim
# removed is already in them, and the list below starts empty: each zero phase
# declares only what it changes relative to whim.
#
# 0 declares nothing: the seed is whim-vim.c byte for byte, built -no-pie, and the
# harnesses must see no difference at all.
#
# 1 declares nothing: -fno-stack-protector changes the code gcc emits around a
# function's locals, not what the editor does.
#
# 2 removes the two warnings check_tty() printed when stdin or stdout was not a
# terminal, the two seconds it then paused, and --ttyfail.  IT DECLARED NOTHING
# UNTIL PHASE 3, and that was true of the old instrument rather than of the
# editor: behaviour.py and exsweep.py ran it `-e -s`, where exmode_active is set
# before check_tty() and its other branch is taken, so no recorded stderr ever
# held the warnings, and termcheck.py drives a pty where both streams ARE
# terminals.  The instrument phase 3 installs runs the editor on a pipe, which is
# exactly where those warnings were printed, so the difference is visible now and
# is declared where it happened: every one of the 102 cases, 109 of the 111 command
# rows (:stop and :suspend are skipped and run nothing) and 13 of the 30 command
# lines differ in their stderr and in nothing else -- measured with
# tools/zcompare.py, which is what "and in nothing else" means here.
2     stderr-moved
#
# 3 declares nothing of its own.  It changes no source: it replaces the
# instrument, and the line above is the difference that instrument can now see.
#
# 4 removes Ex mode, silent mode and the four options that reach them.  Six records
# move and every one of them is a way IN to Ex mode: the two keys, and the three
# command lines the parser accepted for it.  `key_Q` and `key_gQ` drew "Entering Ex
# mode.  Type \"visual\" to go to Normal mode." and now beep once, from nv_error and
# from nv_g_cmd's default; `-e`, `-E` and `-v` are mainerr(ME_UNKNOWN_OPTION) like
# any other unknown letter, and `-e -s` with them, because `-s` was only ever read
# after `-e` had been.
#
# `-s` ALONE IS NOT DECLARED, and the plan's P3 row over-declares it: `case 's'`
# set silent mode only `if (exmode_active)` and called mainerr() otherwise, so a
# bare `-s` was an unknown option before this phase and is the same unknown option
# after it.  Measured: its record is byte-identical.  Nothing else moves -- not the
# other 26 command lines, not the 111 Ex-command rows, not the four pty scenarios,
# not the terminal table, and not one of the other 100 screen cases.
#
# TWO OF THE FOUR WOULD BE ABSORBED BY `stderr-moved` and are named anyway.  `-e`
# and `-e -s` exited 1 with an empty stream before this phase and exit 1 with an
# empty stream after it; only their stderr moved, which the line above already
# excuses everywhere.  They are ways INTO Ex mode and this is the phase that closes
# them, so the list says so rather than letting a dimension cover it -- and if a
# later phase ever drops `stderr-moved`, these two are already declared.
4     case:key_Q case:key_gQ
      argv:-e argv:-E argv:-e_-s argv:-v
#
# 5 leaves the command line as `+{command}` and `-T {term}`.  Three ways of naming
# something to edit go -- a file argument, a bare `-` and the `--` that made every
# word after it a file name -- and each becomes what every other unknown word
# already was, mainerr(ME_UNKNOWN_OPTION).  Six of the 30 command lines move and
# every one of them names a file or a stream:
#
#   `-` was the one row that BLOCKED: the editor read the keystroke file as buffer
#   text, closed fd 0 and waited on fd 2 for keys that never came (tools/zargv.py
#   says so).  It is now `Unknown option argument: "-"`, exit 1, and the row costs
#   the timeout no longer.
#   `f.txt` and `+q! f.txt` opened a buffer and drew a screen; both are exit 1 with
#   an empty stream now.
#   `--` and `-- +q!` ended the options, so `+q!` after `--` was a FILE NAME and not
#   a command -- which is why the two rows differ from each other in the baselines
#   and agree now.
#   `f.txt g.txt` is declared although `stderr-moved` would absorb it: it exited 1
#   with an empty stream before, for `Too many edit arguments: "g.txt"`, and exits 1
#   with an empty stream now for `Unknown option argument: "f.txt"`.  It is the
#   two-file-argument row and this is the phase that removes file arguments, so the
#   list says so rather than letting a dimension cover it.
#
# Nothing else moves, and the screen corpus is the reason it cannot: every case
# seeds itself by TYPING under `'paste'` (ZERO-PLAN.md 2d), so not one of the 102
# passes a file argument.  Measured: all 102 cases, all 111 Ex-command rows, the
# four pty scenarios and the terminal table are identical, as are the other 24
# command lines -- including every `+{command}` form and all three `-T` spellings.
5     argv:- argv:-- argv:f.txt argv:f.txt_g.txt argv:+q!_f.txt argv:--_+q!
#
# 6 removes every way to write a file: the six Ex commands `:write :wq :xit :exit
# :update :saveas`, and with them the nineteen functions the sweep finds unreachable
# once their rows are gone.  TWO SCREEN CASES AND SIX COMMAND ROWS MOVE, and the two
# kinds of movement are different:
#
#   `cmd_write` types `:write` WITH NO FILE NAME, so what the baselines hold is an
#   editor that FAILED to write, `E32: No file name`.  It is `E492: Not an editor
#   command: write` now.  That is the whole of what the corpus can see of this
#   phase, which is why pipes/zero6-check.sh runs both binaries in a directory it
#   keeps and requires the old one to leave a file on the disk.
#   `zz_key` moves because `nv_Zet` ran the command string `"x"`.  ZZ IS ZQ NOW, by
#   the user's decision, so the string is `"q!"`: one bell becomes none, E32 goes,
#   and the record loses two snapshots.  It moves whatever is done here -- left
#   alone, `ZZ` would answer E492 for a command that no longer exists -- so this
#   phase owns it.  ZERO-PLAN.md gives it to the `:q` phase; that row is annotated.
#   the six ref-excmds.txt rows do not change message: they CEASE TO EXIST.
#   tools/zexcmds.py enumerates the table through create_cmdidxs.names(), which
#   reads 105 names where it read 111, so there is no row to compare.
#
# Nothing else moves.  Measured with tools/zcompare.py: the other 100 screen cases,
# the other 105 command rows, all 30 command lines, the four pty scenarios and the
# terminal table are identical -- which includes `:q` on a modified buffer (E37,
# byte-identical: `check_changed` stays and is the `:q` phase's), `:read`, `:edit`,
# `:file` and CTRL-G.
6     case:cmd_write case:zz_key
      write wq xit exit update saveas
#
# 7 removes the way to read one: the Ex command `:read`, with the `:r !cmd` arm
# that was the last caller of the filter and shell plumbing whim left as stubs.
# TWO SCREEN CASES AND ONE COMMAND ROW MOVE:
#
#   `cmd_read` types `:read` WITH NO FILE NAME, so what the baselines hold is an
#   editor that FAILED to read, `E32: No file name` -- the shape phase 6 left
#   `:write` in.  It is `E492: Not an editor command: read` now.
#   `read_cmd_gone` types `:r !echo piped`, which whim's stub answered with
#   `E319: Sorry, the command is not available in this version`.  It is E492 now:
#   one snapshot fewer and the same single bell either side.
#   the ref-excmds.txt row `read` does not change message: it CEASES TO EXIST,
#   because tools/zexcmds.py enumerates the table and there are 104 names where
#   there were 105.
#
# `filter_gone` IS NOT DECLARED, and the plan's P6 row over-declares it: `:!` has
# not existed since whim, so `:%!sort` already answered E492 on the input binary
# and its record is byte-identical.  What this phase removes is the code behind a
# command that was already gone.
#
# Nothing else moves, and the corpus is the reason it cannot: every one of the 102
# cases types its own text and names no file, so not one of them can read one.
# Measured with tools/zcompare.py: the other 100 screen cases, the other 103
# command rows, all 30 command lines, the four pty scenarios and the terminal table
# are identical -- `:redo`, `:redraw`, `:registers` and `:reg` among them, which is
# the inheritance check a removed name asks for.
7     case:cmd_read case:read_cmd_gone
      read
#
# 8 removes every way to name something else to edit: the five Ex commands `:edit
# :enew :ex :visual :view` -- all one handler -- and the four Normal-mode keys that
# do the same thing from the buffer's own text, `gf gF [f ]f`.  TWO SCREEN CASES AND
# FIVE COMMAND ROWS MOVE, and the two kinds of movement are different:
#
#   `cmd_edit` types `:edit` WITH NO FILE NAME, so what the baselines hold is an
#   editor that got as far as check_changed() and refused, `E37: No write since last
#   change (add ! to override)`.  It is `E492: Not an editor command: edit` now.
#   `key_gf` presses `gf` on a word that names nothing, so what the baselines hold
#   is `E447: Can't find file "nosuchfile" in path` -- an editor that LOOKED.  The
#   key beeps from nv_g_cmd's `default: clearopbeep` now, where every other unused
#   `g` key does: the record loses one snapshot and keeps its ONE BELL either side,
#   so what moved is the screen and the snapshot count and not the bell.
#   the five ref-excmds.txt rows do not change message: they CEASE TO EXIST.
#   tools/zexcmds.py enumerates the table through create_cmdidxs' names(), which
#   reads 99 names where it read 104 -- and 99 is under the floor that tool had, so
#   this phase lowers it to 80 (ZERO-PLAN.md decision 8) in its own commit.
#
# Nothing else moves.  Measured with tools/zcompare.py: the other 100 screen cases,
# the other 94 command rows, all 30 command lines, the four pty scenarios and the
# terminal table are identical -- `:earlier`, `:vglobal`, `:vmap` and `:file` among
# them, which is the inheritance check five removed names ask for, and `:q` on a
# modified buffer (still E37: check_changed stays and is the `:q` phase's).
#
# THE CORPUS SEES TWO CASES AND NEITHER OPENS A FILE, which is why pipes/zero8-check.sh
# runs both binaries: `:e! keys`, `:ex! keys`, `:visual! keys` and `:view! keys` all
# load a file on the input binary, `:view` alone making `:set ro?` answer `readonly`,
# and `:enew!` empties the buffer -- that is the whole of do_exedit, measured from
# outside, and it is the only evidence that what went was opening a file rather than
# two error messages.
8     case:cmd_edit case:key_gf
      edit enew ex view visual
